I'm joined today by Bill goldsmith who's  professor emeritus of city and regional  planning at Cornell University and also  the author of saving our cities a  progressive plan to transform urban  America bill it's so great to talk to  you and you know during the presidential  election between Donald Trump and  Hillary Clinton  we saw cities and urban centers referred  to in a lot of different ways it was  highly racialized in particular the way  that Donald Trump referred to big cities  and urban areas and there was also a lot  of discussion around cities with  relation to poverty so let's first take  the broadest step back that we can when  the discussion of cities become this  racialized good question great question  so let me bring it in two parts  when did the discussion become  racialized but maybe before that when  did cities become so remarkably  racialized we go back 40 or 50 or even  60 years to the post-world War two ERA  we discover that during the vast  suburbanization that created our  metropolitan areas federal policy  actually legally officially in effect  prohibited the mixing of races in areas  that received federal housing subsidies  bank loan guarantees through the Federal  Housing Administration this was a  uniformly - throughout the United States  it was prohibited finally by the Supreme  Court in the 1950s and it also  influenced totally housing policy for  public housing so was for private  suburban development and public housing  policy private suburban development was  restricted almost entirely to white  populations and housing policy located  black and poor and Latino and other  minority populations Chinese in San  Francisco for example in specific  housing projects restricted to them  alone so federal and local policy really  dictated  sharp shot segregation of races the  United States then during the Kennedy  and Johnson presidencies with lots and  lots of urban program there were  attempts to break this down but they  succeeded very little against massive  white flight so what we get by the time  we enter the election process this year  is a country that's just deeply deeply  divided between essentially dark-skinned  cities and poor cities cities with poor  inhabitants despite spikes of tremendous  wealth and places like Manhattan or a  central Seattle or Portland or San  Francisco  and suburbs that are largely white and  middle-class now it's true that by now a  majority of poor people by census  standards live in what we call suburbs  that is areas outside what some call the  white noose that strangles cities but  those are also often known as cities or  urban areas in our language and they're  deprived so they have inferior schools  inferior public services lower tax  incomes even though they have very very  high tax rates so this this sort of  stratification gets to be recognized as  Trump I think quite vulgar Lee refers to  all black people as though they're all  poor even though very very large numbers  of middle class and live in middle-class  suburbs and along those lines bill  because there's there's a you've touched  on something which was sort of going to  be what I wanted to get into next  there's this assumption often that  cities are the root of many of society's  problems in terms of being associated  with very high crime rates and drug  issues etc can you talk to us a little  bit about how we should really think  about that and one thing that seems  missing to me from  that conversation is by their very  nature when you have higher  concentrations of people you are going  to have dynamics that are different than  in rural and suburban areas and I don't  feel like that's often accounted for  when cities are just criticized yeah  well you're right and you seen what I've  written so you know I agree the the the  so there's a couple of issues here first  of all it's not at all clear that City  crime rates are higher than rural crime  rates and it is absolutely untrue that  rates of drug use in cities are higher  than rates of drug use in either suburbs  or small towns or rural areas there is  simply no evidence whatsoever and there  are massive surveys undertaken every  year by good solid statistical agencies  University of Michigan Survey Research  Center for example working for the Drug  Enforcement Agency and the census and so  forth that show that drug use rates are  pretty uniform across racial groups  they're somewhat lower among Asians  they're somewhat higher among Native  American Indians but the two three big  groups that are always examined blacks  whites Hispanics or Latinos the the drug  alcohol tobacco uses very up and down a  little bit across his not very much  similarly they don't vary much by a  location what does vary is where police  can find as you suggest here relatively  dense concentrations of people where  it's much easier to make large numbers  of arrests or for that matter it may be  easier to find locations where drugs are  sold  of course when they're sold they're sold  by city residents to people who come in  from the suburbs to buy them I mean  that's where the money is the big money  is of course in selling drugs to people  who have lots of money and that's  predominantly in the United States  whites so the drug problem is  principally a white problem that  were though which does as all evidence  suggests nothing whatsoever to reduce to  either drug sales or drug use prosecutes  people who happen to live in poor  neighborhoods of central cities the  reasons for that are very complex they  have to do with ideology with racism  with this sort of theme that drug  problems are always someone else's  problems unlike alcohol problems where  everybody has an Uncle Louie who's an  alcoholic and it's a problem at family  gatherings and weddings and so forth and  we sort of treat them as though it's a  behavioral problem a mental problem  something to be tolerated but controlled  whereas with minority drug use we say  these are criminals they're thugs  they're people ruining our world it's  simply not true but it's just it's been  worked into our ideology so thoroughly  this begins a long long time ago with  Asian exclusion laws with the assumption  that drug abuse in cities was something  brought in by the Chinese and then  attempts to export Mexicans very early  on who are accused of bringing marijuana  into the country this is I'm talking  about 4050 years ago even longer and  then of course the blame entirely that  the whole drug problem is a problem of  ghettos in American cities when it isn't  at all the problem is that the drug war  creates tremendous violence reduces  opportunities removes potentially  productive men from their homes and in  prison ISM in what is now widely  recognized as insanely large numbers of  people who haven't committed violent  crimes but go to prison and learn how to  commit violent crimes I'm myself  involved in a prison education program  that a group of Cornell professor  started running in the 1990s when the  Congress  and President Clinton cut funding for  college scholarships Pell grants to  prisons 1993 they just jumped in to  begin teaching in prisons and the  results have been absolutely phenomenal  we now work in for upstate New York  bad news prisons where prisoners are  just dying to get in and come to class  study hard that people who teach in the  prison simply love teaching there  because the prisoners are so responsive  and study so hard it's true they don't  have a lot else to do but it just  changes the tone sometimes in the entire  prison so so I'm combining all of this  putting the drug war or or the prison  expansion the enormous profits by  private corporations that supply prisons  a huge influx of sort of warfighting  material into police departments all the  fancy equipment and vehicles they have  the new kinds of crowd dispersal bombs  these flash bombs  ya know in the interest of because we  just have a limited time and there were  so many things I wanted to get to and  I'll have to sort of pick and choose  what we'll have time for you started to  get into the economic aspect of this in  the book you actually talked more about  the importance of inequality in cities  than just poverty itself an inequality  of course more of a relative measure  than poverty make that case why is  inequality something that we should be  looking closely at in relation to  poverty well you know that's that's  actually a great question I'm  remembering that in 1930 Justice  Brandeis said we can have democracy or  we can have an equality but we can't  have both those aren't his exact words  but it's very close in the last 20 years  as we all know because of the wall  street 1% protests we have funded  through our tax policies and our  expenditure policies a tiny minority  1/10 of 1% of the population  maybe and  very very giant corporations who have  just walked away with all of the  productivity gains in the last 20 or 30  years and they've taken money away from  just those people who I suspect in large  numbers voted for Donald Trump yesterday  that inequality comes on top of the  poverty for people at the bottom they're  not directly related but they're very  closely related I think it the  inequality in our cities which shows up  in the statistics but it's inequality  nationally as well  drives politics that leads us to ignore  the needs of the bulk of the population  this is the sort of lower ends of the  working-class and those who don't vote  at all who are lots of very poor people  all over the country and they're  predominantly concentrated in in cities  you know the the if you look at the  numbers something like eighty percent of  the US population lives in metropolitan  areas with populations larger than  200,000 people right so this this claim  that everybody's out in the rural areas  just isn't true large majorities of  Americans live either in cities or in  the suburbs immediately surrounding  cities they may have been small towns  before but they're really urbanized now  so this inequality leads us to ignore  the needs of large numbers of our  population and make things okay for  those of us who are lucky enough to have  good incomes and really okay for that  tiny group of people who have enormous  lehigh incomes and it's pretty clear to  me that that's a major piece of what's  driving on sort of insane politics today  yeah I think you're absolutely right  about that we've been speaking about the  book saving our cities a progressive  plan to transform urban America will be  very important over the next four years  under president elect Trump we've been  speaking with the books author Bill  Goldsmith thanks so much for talking to  us  about this thank you very much I enjoyed  talking to you
 

